---
import { calculateForgetScenario, type ForgetScenario } from "../lib/recurrent-memory-engine";
import type { Mode } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { scenario: ForgetScenario; mode: Mode }
const { scenario, mode } = Astro.props;
if (scenario.id !== "unmarked-boundary" || scenario.presets.length !== 3) {
  throw new Error("The forget-gate chapter requires its reviewed three-preset comparison.");
}
const trace = calculateForgetScenario(scenario);
const decimals = mode === "beginner" ? 3 : 6;
const format = (value: number) => value.toFixed(decimals);
const first = trace.presets[0]!;
const last = trace.presets[2]!;
---

<section class:list={["mass-ledger recurrent-ledger forget-retention-ledger", `recurrent-ledger--${mode}`]} aria-labelledby="retention-ledger-heading">
  <header class="ledger-header">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">Synthetic example · one forward update</p><h2 id="retention-ledger-heading">Same write. Different retention.</h2></div>
    <p class="ledger-folio">Forget-gated cell · 2000<br />Earlier versions · 1999</p>
  </header>
  <div class="ledger-prompt"><p><strong>Predict first.</strong> {scenario.prompt} Does near-reset make the resulting state zero?</p></div>
  <div class="trace-intro">
    <p>{mode === "beginner" ? "Read these as three alternatives for the same moment. First keep a fraction of the old activation, then add the same new write." : "A one-step controlled contrast isolates the retained term. Previous state, candidate, and input-gate activation are identical in every preset. The calculation has no output-gate, eligibility-trace, or parameter-update simulation."}</p>
    <p class="mt-3">They are not three successive steps. These inputs stay fixed:</p>
    <dl class="retention-invariants mt-3"><div><dt>Previous state</dt><dd>{scenario.previousState}</dd></div><div><dt>Candidate</dt><dd>{scenario.candidate}</dd></div><div><dt>Input gate</dt><dd>{scenario.inputGate}</dd></div><div><dt>New write · candidate × input gate</dt><dd>{format(trace.inputGatedWrite)}</dd></div></dl>
    <p class="provenance-note mt-3">Authored gate activations, not learned parameters or paper results. The populated incoming state is a teaching choice; journal equation 7 initializes a sequence at zero. In a trained network, the gate is computed from source-unit outputs through learned weights. The three presets do not train or change those weights.</p>
    <nav class="preset-index mt-3" aria-label="Inspect a retention preset">{trace.presets.map((preset) => <a href={`#retention-${preset.id}`}>{preset.label}</a>)}</nav>
  </div>
  {trace.presets.map((preset, index) => <section class="trace-history" aria-labelledby={`retention-${preset.id}`}>
    <p class="eyebrow">Alternative {index + 1} of 3 · same incoming state</p><h3 id={`retention-${preset.id}`} class="mt-3">{preset.label}</h3>
    <table class="trace-table retention-table mt-3">
      <caption>{preset.label} · one forward update · {decimals} decimals</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Read in order</th><th scope="col">Activation value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Forget gate</th><td>{format(preset.forgetGate)}</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Retained old state</th><td>{format(preset.retainedContribution)}</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Same new write</th><td>{format(trace.inputGatedWrite)}</td></tr>
        <tr class="trace-result"><th scope="row">Resulting state</th><td>{format(preset.resultingState)}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    {mode === "expert" && <p class="provenance-note mt-3">{preset.forgetGate} × {scenario.previousState} + {format(trace.inputGatedWrite)} = {format(preset.resultingState)}. Retention is applied before addition.</p>}
  </section>)}
  <div class="trace-history">
    <details class="prediction-reveal"><summary>Check the retention prediction</summary>
      <p>Near retain keeps {format(first.retainedContribution)} of the old activation; near reset keeps {format(last.retainedContribution)}. Both add {format(trace.inputGatedWrite)}. Near reset therefore produces {format(last.resultingState)}, not zero.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">Even an idealized gate of zero would remove only the old-state contribution before the new write. A gate of 0.1 still retains a tenth. “Near retain” at 0.9 also loses a tenth; it is not the original fixed multiplier of 1.0.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Check the activation-versus-parameters distinction</summary>
      <p>The carried activation changes. The comparison does not erase learned weights, train new weights, or demonstrate catastrophic forgetting. Input gating controls the new write; output gating controls exposure; the forget gate controls how much old activation enters the update.</p>
      <p class="mt-3"><strong>Explain the cause:</strong> why can the state remain nonzero after most of its old contribution is removed?</p>
    </details>
  </div>
  <div class="trace-comparison"><p class="eyebrow">Mechanism boundary · 1999/2000</p><p class="mt-3">Retain, then write; expose through the existing output gate. No cell-state-to-gate peephole in this stage. These alternatives show activation arithmetic, not a learned boundary detector, a backward gradient, or a language-model score.</p></div>
</section>
